PART B  pi-parity lemma:  a0 = q (2 pi)^j c H_Lambda, q algebraic, j integer  =>  kappa^2 = (8 pi/3) q^2 (2 pi)^(2j) is transcendental
      j =  -2.0   kappa^2 = (8 pi/3)(2 pi)^-4  ->  algebraic of degree none found (deg<=6, coeff<=1000)
      j =  -1.5   kappa^2 = (8 pi/3)(2 pi)^-3  ->  algebraic of degree none found (deg<=6, coeff<=1000)
      j =  -1.0   kappa^2 = (8 pi/3)(2 pi)^-2  ->  algebraic of degree none found (deg<=6, coeff<=1000)
      j =  -0.5   kappa^2 = (8 pi/3)(2 pi)^-1  ->  algebraic of degree 1
      j =   0.0   kappa^2 = (8 pi/3)(2 pi)^0  ->  algebraic of degree none found (deg<=6, coeff<=1000)
      j =   0.5   kappa^2 = (8 pi/3)(2 pi)^1  ->  algebraic of degree none found (deg<=6, coeff<=1000)
      j =   1.0   kappa^2 = (8 pi/3)(2 pi)^2  ->  algebraic of degree none found (deg<=6, coeff<=1000)
      j =   1.5   kappa^2 = (8 pi/3)(2 pi)^3  ->  algebraic of degree none found (deg<=6, coeff<=1000)
  [OK] B1  for every INTEGER j (even j2) no integer relation of degree <= 6 (coefficients <= 1000) exists for kappa^2 (as Lindemann demands: pi is transcendental)
  [OK] B2  the relation exists exactly when the power of pi in kappa^2 vanishes, i.e. j = -1/2 (a0^2 proportional to 1/(2 pi), i.e. to 1/A or to G rho): j2 = -1 -> degree 1
  [OK] B3  the framework is that case:  a0 = (sqrt3/4)(2 pi)^(-1/2) c H_Lambda  (q = sqrt3/4, j = -1/2)  gives kappa = 1/2 exactly

